"Numerical agreement with a fixed reference does not by itself establish that a downstream scientific interpretation is robust to small input changes. We investigate this distinction for Bader charges derived from compressed electronic densities using 6,343 frozen reconstructions of 254 bulk and slab systems, an additive 1,332-reconstruction common-tight-ladder completion, and an earlier frozen 63-system external confirmation. QoI Stability Qualification (QSQ) screens reference sensitivity using five fixed-seed perturbations. Equalizing tight-ladder search opportunity preserves strong risk stratification: at $10^{-3}\\,e$, failure to find a numerically passing reconstruction occurs for 3.3% of eligible versus 66.4% of screen-rejected material–codec pairs. We then prospectively test the frozen five-seed gate using 59 pre-registered fresh perturbations per development material (14,986/14,986 successful trials). At the primary $10^{-3}\\,e$ endpoint, fresh-threshold exceedance risk is 1.60% in the 143 admitted materials versus 81.33% in the 111 screen-rejected materials, a 50.83-fold risk ratio under the declared iid-uniform perturbation model. Re-derived Bader charges respond irregularly through basin migration, whereas Hartree-potential error behaves more regularly; realized-distortion matching separates nominal-tolerance effects from residual codec differences. These results establish QSQ as a prospectively validated empirical risk-stratification screen for this stated perturbation model while preserving a strict distinction between reference robustness and fixed-pipeline compression fidelity. Finite perturbation panels do not constitute worst-case stability guarantees."
