"The QSQ perturbation test illustrates a second consequence of the framework: the stability test itself is part of the measurement contract. The archived float32 probe was numerically small but structurally inappropriate because it largely preserved the ordering that drives the on-grid partition. A valid qualification procedure must therefore probe the failure modes of the downstream operator, not merely impose an arbitrarily small perturbation. The five-seed maximum is conservative relative to using any one of those seeds, but not an upper bound on unseen perturbations. The prospective 59-seed test now provides direct empirical evidence that this frozen screen is strongly discriminative for unseen draws from the same declared perturbation model, while also revealing a non-zero 1.60% exceedance rate among accepted trials at the primary threshold. QSQ should therefore be interpreted as a validated risk-stratification screen, not an intrinsic material constant or worst-case certificate. Moreover, separate reference-response and reconstruction-error tests below the same tolerance do not automatically imply a combined robust error below that tolerance; an explicit target and error budget are needed."
